# Copy to .env for local development. Never commit the real .env - it is in
# .gitignore for that reason. In production these come from the host's secret
# manager, never from a file in git.

# Qumo's own database. Separate from CIOS on purpose: the two products share
# no tables and no runtime, so a schema change on one side cannot break the
# other. See docs/qumo-architecture.md.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://qumo_dev:qumo_dev_local_only@localhost:5432/qumo_dev"

# The apex Qumo serves brands under. Every brand is a subdomain of it:
# chicken-licken.qumo.co.za, campari.qumo.co.za. Nothing else about the
# request decides which brand a shopper is looking at, so this is the most
# load-bearing string in the file.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because it is read in proxy.ts, which runs in the Edge
# runtime and is compiled at build time - a value supplied only at runtime
# would not reach it. It is a hostname, not a secret.
#
# Left unset it defaults to "localhost", which is why a fresh checkout runs
# with no configuration: *.localhost resolves to 127.0.0.1 in every current
# browser, so http://chicken-licken.localhost:3000 works with no hosts-file
# editing. In production it must be set BEFORE the build, or every request
# lands on the no-brand page.
NEXT_PUBLIC_QUMO_ROOT_DOMAIN="localhost"

# The brand console lives at app.{root} - app.localhost:3000 locally. It is
# in the reserved-subdomain list in lib/brand/host.ts, so no brand can ever
# register a slug that would claim it.
#
# Signs the shopper session cookie. Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET="replace-with-a-generated-secret"

# Auth.js-style host trust. Only matters once the brand console lands and
# brings a staff login with it; harmless now, and documented here because
# running a production build locally without it fails in a way that reads as
# a broken page rather than a missing variable.
AUTH_TRUST_HOST="true"

# PII protection for shopper phone numbers (lib/security/crypto.ts).
# PHONE_HASH_SECRET: openssl rand -base64 32
# ENCRYPTION_KEY:    openssl rand -base64 32   (must be 32 raw bytes for AES-256)
PHONE_HASH_SECRET="replace-with-a-generated-secret"
ENCRYPTION_KEY="replace-with-a-generated-32-byte-key"

# SMS for shopper login passcodes. Twilio Messaging rather than Twilio
# Verify - the passcode lifecycle (hashing, expiry, attempt cap, single use)
# lives in lib/consumer/otp.ts so it stays testable and provider-independent,
# and swapping to a South African aggregator is one class in
# lib/sms/client.ts. Left empty, a logging implementation prints the passcode
# to the console and the whole login flow runs with no account provisioned.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=""
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=""
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=""

# Authenticates the scheduled call to /api/cron/sweep-sessions, which deletes
# shopper session rows long past expiry. Generate any random string:
# openssl rand -hex 32. Left empty the route always 401s and nothing is ever
# deleted - the safe direction for a URL whose job is deletion.
CRON_SECRET=""

# Where unexpected failures go beyond the log (lib/observability/report.ts).
#
# Left empty, errors are still written to stdout as structured JSON - which
# Vercel captures and indexes, so nothing is lost and no account is needed.
# Set it and each distinct failure also posts once per five minutes to that
# URL. The body leads with a `text` field, so a Slack incoming webhook
# renders it with no adapter in between; anything else can read the `event`
# object beside it.
#
# Sentry would be the third sink and is deliberately not wired up: it wants
# an account and a DSN that do not exist yet, and a half-configured
# integration is worse than an honest gap. Adding it is one more branch in
# reportError().
ERROR_WEBHOOK_URL=""

# Where the till simulator's QR codes point. Development only - /dev/till
# 404s in production regardless of this.
#
# Left empty it uses the address you opened the till on, which is right for
# every local case. Set it to a deployed site and the codes point there
# instead: run `pnpm dev` against the deployed database, open the till on
# your laptop, and scan the QR with a phone. The slip is signed with the
# store's real secret, so the deployed site accepts it - which is how you
# demo a deployed Qumo, since the till itself cannot be deployed.
#
#   QUMO_TILL_ORIGIN="https://chicken-licken.qumo.co.za"
QUMO_TILL_ORIGIN=""

# A fixed passcode for numbers you name, while there is no SMS account.
#
# Shopper login sends a passcode by SMS. With no aggregator account the code
# is written to the server log instead, which is fine for a developer and
# useless for showing the product to anybody. Set both of these and the
# listed numbers can sign in with the code below.
#
# Deliberately a list and a fixed code rather than "any code works". The
# second is the same amount of typing at a demo and hands anyone who finds
# the address the ability to sign in as any number in the country.
#
# Both must be set or the whole thing is off. Numbers can be written
# however is easiest - 082 123 4567, 0821234567, +27821234567 - and the
# code must be six digits. Remove both the day SMS is connected; the build
# warns while they are set.
#
#   DEMO_LOGIN_PHONES="082 123 4567,083 555 0000"
#   DEMO_LOGIN_CODE="123456"
DEMO_LOGIN_PHONES=""
DEMO_LOGIN_CODE=""
